# Keep secrets and build noise out of the image.
#
# `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile means this file is the only thing standing between
# the build context and the image. Two concrete consequences of getting it wrong:
#
#   * A real `.env` would be baked into a layer. Deleting it in a later RUN does
#     not help — image history keeps every layer, so `docker history` would still
#     reveal it. `.gitignore` protects the repo; it does NOT protect the image.
#   * Local virtualenvs / caches would be copied in and then shadowed by the
#     installed packages, which is both slow and a source of "works on my
#     machine" divergence (e.g. a stale .pyc winning over new source).

# --- Secrets ---
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example

# --- Python environment & caches ---
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- Local data ---
*.db
*.sqlite3
var/
uploads/

# --- VCS / tooling ---
.git/
.gitignore
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
docker-compose*.yml

# --- Docs & tests (not needed to run the service) ---
tests/
docs/
README.md
